# @tag: system, gui, rendering, web, offload, capture, gpu
# @cover src/lib/gc_async_mut/gpu/browser_engine/simple_web_render_session.spl 90%
# @cover src/lib/gc_async_mut/gpu/browser_engine/web_render_backend.spl 90%

"""Web showcase document rendered end-to-end under the GPU offload profile.

A rich HTML showcase (headings, paragraphs, styled boxes, flex row, colors,
borders, a transformed box, an animated element) is rendered through the
pure_simple WebRenderBackend with the "auto" Engine2D backend — the full GPU
offload profile (Metal / CUDA / ROCm / Vulkan, then CPU fallbacks) — and the
resulting bitmap is verified with capture evidence:

  - non-empty bitmap of the expected dimensions,
  - deterministic checksum across two identical renders,
  - checksum CHANGES when the document changes (mutation sensitivity),
  - pixel probes for known-color regions (body bg, flex boxes),
  - honest offload/readback provenance: the lane that actually executed is
    asserted, never fabricated (a CPU-family lane must NOT claim
    device_readback — see
    doc/08_tracking/bug/web_render_gpu_backend_provenance_fabricated_2026-06-17.md).

READING THIS RUN'S OUTPUT (the receipt contract)
------------------------------------------------
This suite is titled "full GPU offload", so a green here is read as "the GPU
offload path works". Until 2026-08-05 that reading was unsafe: a private
`_executed_render_backend()` helper silently RETARGETED every example to the
"software" lane whenever its probe did not report `device_readback`, and all 13
examples then passed on the CPU with no skip disclosure anywhere in the output.
The whole suite reported

    showcase_capture resolved=cuda executed=software probe_source= ...
    13 examples, 0 failures

while not one pixel had been near a device — and the single field that could
have disclosed it (`probe_source=`) came back EMPTY, because a module-level
`var` written inside an example is not visible when read back (the same trap
recorded in the sibling parity gate). The retarget is gone. The suite now runs
on the lane the resolver actually names, and every example emits exactly one
greppable receipt on its own line prefix:

    [showcase-lane] <label>: GPU-PROVEN    — device_readback + handle + identity
                                             + a full frame: a device produced
                                             these pixels.
    [showcase-lane] <label>: DEVICE-SERVED — a device presented the frame
                                             (host_cache_after_device_copy /
                                             swapchain_present) with real
                                             credentials, but the strict
                                             device_readback proof was not taken.
    [showcase-lane] <label>: CPU LANE      — the auto profile found no viable
                                             device on this host; this example
                                             proves NOTHING about GPU offload.

Counting rule (ANCHORED — an unanchored grep counts the verdict's own prose and
inflates the total; that is how the sibling gate misread 1 for 0):

    grep -c '^.showcase-lane. '                  -> receipts emitted (expect 13)
    grep -c '^.showcase-lane. .*GPU-PROVEN'      -> strict device proofs
    grep -c '^.showcase-lane. .*DEVICE-SERVED'   -> device-presented frames
    grep -c '^.showcase-lane. .*CPU LANE'        -> examples that prove NOTHING

The closing verdict lines use a DIFFERENT prefix (`[showcase-verdict]`) on
purpose, so they can name the tokens without ever being counted as evidence.

FAIL vs INCONCLUSIVE
--------------------
The sibling parity gate chose "inconclusive, loudly" for a degraded render,
arguing that an absent device is environment rather than defect and that a
permanently-red gate gets muted. That reasoning transfers only HALF way here,
so this suite splits the two cases:

  - The resolver names a CPU lane  -> INCONCLUSIVE, loudly, still green. No
    viable device exists on this host; the layout/pixel/session evidence is
    still real, but the GPU offload claim is unproven and every receipt says so.
    This keeps the suite runnable device-free, which is why deleting the
    retarget outright was not the fix.

  - The resolver names a GPU lane and the frame comes back CPU-served -> HARD
    FAIL. That is not a host condition. `detect_best_backend_viable()` commits
    "only to a PROVABLY working backend", so a named device that does not
    deliver is the resolver breaking its own contract, and it is exactly the
    state the retarget was hiding: on this host (TITAN RTX + RTX A6000, Vulkan
    1.4) auto resolved to `cuda`, whose every real web frame returned
    `source=cpu_fallback handle=0 identity=0`, because CudaBackend.set_clip only
    mutates the CPU mirror and every page with text paints under a clip. The
    lane that could serve the frame on-device was never reached. Fixed in
    `Engine2D.probe_backend_viable` (fill + CLIPPED fill + blit, all on-device);
    auto now names a device lane whose showcase frame reads back
    `host_cache_after_device_copy` with real credentials, bit-identical to
    the CPU ground truth. This gate is what keeps that fixed.

## Purpose and audience
Purpose: resolves the auto profile to one concrete offload lane
Audience: compiler and tooling engineers who maintain this spec

"""

# @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001

use std.spec.*
use std.common.web.browser_renderer_protocol.{browser_text_input_overlay_empty}
use std.gc_async_mut.web.browser_session.{
    BrowserRenderRevisions,
    BrowserRenderSnapshot
}
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.browser_engine.simple_web_render_session.{
    SimpleWebRenderSession
}
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.browser_engine.web_render_backend.{
    web_render_backend, web_render_backend_names
}
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.browser_engine.simple_web_renderer.{
    simple_web_resolved_engine2d_backend_name,
    simple_web_render_html_to_readback_result_with_engine2d_backend,
    simple_web_render_html_to_readback_result_with_engine2d_backend_at_time
}
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.browser_engine.backend_screenshot_capture.{
    backend_capture_ok
}
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.browser_engine.simple_web_html_layout_renderer_foundation.{
    simple_web_layout_set_render_budget_floor_ms
}

val SHOW_W: i32 = 96
val SHOW_H: i32 = 72

# Palette pinned by the showcase CSS below (0xAARRGGBB).
val COLOR_BODY_BG: u32 = 0xFF1A1A2Eu32
val COLOR_BOX_BLUE: u32 = 0xFF2563EBu32
val COLOR_BOX_GREEN: u32 = 0xFF22C55Eu32
val COLOR_BOX_AMBER: u32 = 0xFFF59E0Bu32
val COLOR_BOX_MUTATED: u32 = 0xFFDC2626u32

fn _showcase_html() -> text:
    "<html><head><style>" +
    "html,body { margin:0; background-color:#1a1a2e; color:#e0e0ff; }" +
    # compact type: default h1/p margins + 2em h1 would push the flex row
    # and pulse element below the 96x72 viewport (h1 alone covers ~45 rows)
    "h1 { margin:0; font-size:12px; background-color:#16213e; " +
    "color:#a0c4ff; padding:2px; }" +
    "p { margin:0; font-size:8px; color:#cbd5e1; }" +
    ".row { display:flex; width:96px; }" +
    ".box-a { width:28px; height:18px; background-color:#2563eb; " +
    "border:2px solid #533483; }" +
    ".box-b { width:28px; height:18px; background-color:#22c55e; }" +
    ".box-c { width:28px; height:18px; background-color:#f59e0b; " +
    "transform:translate(2px,1px); }" +
    ".pulse { width:24px; height:10px; background-color:#ef4444; " +
    "animation-name:pulse; animation-duration:1s; }" +
    "</style></head><body>" +
    "<h1>Showcase</h1>" +
    "<p>GPU offload capture evidence</p>" +
    "<div class='row'>" +
    "<div class='box-a'></div>" +
    "<div class='box-b'></div>" +
    "<div class='box-c'></div>" +
    "</div>" +
    "<div class='pulse'></div>" +
    "</body></html>"

fn _mutated_html() -> text:
    _showcase_html().replace("#2563eb", "#dc2626")

fn _count_color(pixels: [u32], color: u32) -> i32:
    var n = 0
    var i = 0
    while i < pixels.len():
        if pixels[i] == color:
            n = n + 1
        i = i + 1
    n

fn _diffcount(a: [u32], b: [u32]) -> i32:
    if a.len() != b.len():
        return -1
    var n = 0
    var i = 0
    while i < a.len():
        if a[i] != b[i]:
            n = n + 1
        i = i + 1
    n

fn _row_has_both(pixels: [u32], left: u32, right: u32) -> bool:
    """True when some scanline carries BOTH colors (flex-row side-by-side)."""
    var y = 0
    while y < SHOW_H:
        var saw_left = false
        var saw_right = false
        var x = 0
        while x < SHOW_W:
            val p = pixels[y * SHOW_W + x]
            if p == left:
                saw_left = true
            if p == right:
                saw_right = true
            x = x + 1
        if saw_left and saw_right:
            return true
        y = y + 1
    false

# The default 10s WEB_RENDER_BUDGET_MS wall-clock render budget is sized for
# compiled execution. Under the tree-walking interpreter (the engine
# `simple test` runs on) the 12-node showcase legitimately needs more:
# compute_styles alone trips the budget at ~node 5 of 12, silently leaving
# the remaining nodes on default styles so ONLY the canvas background paints
# (uniform 0xFF1A1A2E frame — exactly the degraded mode the foundation module
# documents). Raising the platform floor via this PUBLIC calibration API is
# the sanctioned opt-in for showcase evidence capture (see
# simple_web_html_layout_renderer_foundation.spl, WEB_RENDER_BUDGET_MS notes);
# the budget still expires past the floored deadline, so this is not a bypass.
fn _arm_showcase_budget():
    simple_web_layout_set_render_budget_floor_ms(900000)

val SHOW_PIXELS: i32 = SHOW_W * SHOW_H

# Number of examples this spec DECLARES, so the closing verdict can state its
# own denominator and adding an example without a receipt is a visible edit
# rather than silent drift.
val SHOWCASE_EXAMPLES_DECLARED: i32 = 13

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Honest lane vocabulary — identical to the sibling parity gate's, so the two
# suites classify a readback source the same way.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Sources that mean a real device produced or presented the pixels.
fn _source_is_device(source: text) -> bool:
    source == "device_readback" or source == "host_cache_after_device_present" or source == "host_cache_after_device_copy" or
        source == "swapchain_present"

# Sources that mean the frame was served by the CPU. A frame carrying one of
# these may NEVER be reported as GPU-rendered.
fn _source_is_cpu(source: text) -> bool:
    source == "cpu_mirror" or source == "cpu_fallback"

# Sources that mean the device produced nothing at all this attempt.
fn _source_is_no_frame(source: text) -> bool:
    source == "completion_unknown" or source == "readback_failed" or
        source == "not_requested" or source == "device_identity_unknown"

fn _source_is_known(source: text) -> bool:
    _source_is_device(source) or _source_is_cpu(source) or _source_is_no_frame(source)

# The campaign invariant: a frame may be labelled GPU-rendered only when the
# readback proves a device produced it.
fn _device_proven(source: text, handle: i64, identity: i64, pixel_count: i64) -> bool:
    source == "device_readback" and handle > 0 and identity > 0 and
        pixel_count == SHOW_PIXELS.to_i64()

fn _is_cpu_family(lane: text) -> bool:
    lane == "software" or lane == "cpu" or lane == "cpu_simd"

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Executed-lane evidence.
#
# NOTE: deliberately NOT memoized in a module-level `var`. Every cache this
# spec used to keep (`_executed_backend_cache`, `_probe_source_cache`,
# `_base_px_cache`, `_mutated_px_cache`) silently did not work: a module `var`
# written inside an example is not visible when read back, which is why the
# old run printed `probe_source=` EMPTY in the very example that had just set
# it. Each example therefore derives its own evidence, exactly like the
# sibling gates carry per-example receipts instead of a cross-example tally.
#
# The probe renders an empty document at the SHOWCASE size, so the frame-size
# half of the device-proven predicate is a real check rather than a probe-sized
# one, and the cost stays small.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
class _LaneEvidence:
    lane: text
    source: text
    handle: i64
    identity: i64
    pixel_count: i64

fn _lane_evidence() -> _LaneEvidence:
    _arm_showcase_budget()
    val lane = simple_web_resolved_engine2d_backend_name(SHOW_W, SHOW_H, "auto")
    val probe = simple_web_render_html_to_readback_result_with_engine2d_backend(
        "<html><body></body></html>", SHOW_W, SHOW_H, "auto"
    )
    val rb = probe.readback
    _LaneEvidence(
        lane: lane, source: rb.source, handle: rb.backend_handle,
        identity: rb.device_identity, pixel_count: rb.pixels.len().to_i64()
    )

# Honest-provenance invariants. These hold for EVERY outcome on EVERY lane and
# are asserted unconditionally — this is where the spec keeps its teeth. A
# device label without a handle/identity, or a CPU frame carrying device
# credentials, is the fabricated-provenance defect and fails here regardless of
# which backend happened to serve the frame.
fn _assert_provenance_invariants(label: text, ev: _LaneEvidence):
    if not _source_is_known(ev.source):
        print "[showcase-provenance] {label}: UNKNOWN READBACK SOURCE '{ev.source}' — outside the honest vocabulary, failing closed"
    expect(_source_is_known(ev.source)).to_equal(true)
    if ev.source == "device_readback":
        expect(ev.handle).to_be_greater_than(0)
        expect(ev.identity).to_be_greater_than(0)
        expect(ev.pixel_count).to_equal(SHOW_PIXELS.to_i64())
    if _source_is_cpu(ev.source):
        expect(_source_is_device(ev.source)).to_equal(false)
        expect(_device_proven(ev.source, ev.handle, ev.identity, ev.pixel_count)).to_equal(false)
    # A CPU-family lane may never present a device source, in either direction.
    if _is_cpu_family(ev.lane):
        expect(_source_is_device(ev.source)).to_equal(false)

# Emit this example's single lane receipt and enforce the fail-vs-inconclusive
# split documented in the module header. Returns true when a device genuinely
# served the frame.
fn _report_lane(label: text, ev: _LaneEvidence) -> bool:
    _assert_provenance_invariants(label, ev)
    if _device_proven(ev.source, ev.handle, ev.identity, ev.pixel_count):
        print "[showcase-lane] {label}: GPU-PROVEN — a device produced this frame (lane={ev.lane} source={ev.source} handle={ev.handle} identity={ev.identity} pixels={ev.pixel_count})"
        return true
    if _source_is_device(ev.source) and (ev.handle > 0 or ev.identity > 0):
        print "[showcase-lane] {label}: DEVICE-SERVED — a device presented this frame but the strict device_readback proof was not taken (lane={ev.lane} source={ev.source} handle={ev.handle} identity={ev.identity} pixels={ev.pixel_count})"
        return true
    if _is_cpu_family(ev.lane):
        print "[showcase-lane] {label}: CPU LANE — the auto profile found no viable device on this host (lane={ev.lane} source={ev.source}); this example proves NOTHING about GPU offload"
        return false
    # A GPU lane was NAMED and did not deliver. Not a host condition: the
    # resolver commits only to a provably working backend, so this is the
    # routing defect the old silent "software" retarget used to hide.
    print "[showcase-lane] {label}: LANE INTEGRITY FAILURE — the auto profile named GPU lane '{ev.lane}' but the frame did not come from it (source={ev.source} handle={ev.handle} identity={ev.identity}); a named device that serves a CPU frame, or no frame at all, is a routing defect, not a host condition"
    expect(_source_is_device(ev.source)).to_equal(true)
    false

fn _render_showcase_pixels(html: text) -> [u32]:
    _arm_showcase_budget()
    val backend = web_render_backend("pure_simple", SHOW_W, SHOW_H)
    # The lane the resolver NAMES, never a silent retarget. Verified
    # 2026-08-05: on the resolved device lane this frame is bit-identical to
    # the software ground truth (checksum 413538218, unique=18, nonbg=5565),
    # so honest lane targeting costs no pixel evidence.
    backend.render_html_to_pixels(html, "auto")

fn _base_pixels() -> [u32]:
    _render_showcase_pixels(_showcase_html())

fn _mutated_pixels() -> [u32]:
    _render_showcase_pixels(_mutated_html())

fn _snapshot(
    generation: i64, document_revision: i64, style_revision: i64,
    resource_revision: i64, html: text?
) -> BrowserRenderSnapshot:
    _arm_showcase_budget()
    BrowserRenderSnapshot(
        document_generation: generation,
        revisions: BrowserRenderRevisions(
            document_revision: document_revision,
            style_revision: style_revision,
            resource_revision: resource_revision
        ),
        document_html: html,
        resources_changed: false
    )

describe "Web showcase document under the full GPU offload profile":

    it "resolves the auto profile to one concrete offload lane":
        step("Verify: resolves the auto profile to one concrete offload lane")
        # @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001
        val ev = _lane_evidence()
        _report_lane("resolve", ev)
        val resolved = ev.lane
        # honest lane report — the lane that will actually execute below
        print "showcase_offload_lane={resolved}"
        assert_false(resolved == "")
        assert_false(resolved == "auto")
        val known = (
            resolved == "metal" or resolved == "cuda" or
            resolved == "rocm" or resolved == "vulkan" or
            resolved == "opencl" or resolved == "opengl" or
            resolved == "intel" or resolved == "webgpu" or
            resolved == "qualcomm" or resolved == "directx" or
            resolved == "baremetal" or resolved == "virtio_gpu" or
            _is_cpu_family(resolved)
        )
        assert_true(known)
        val names = web_render_backend_names()
        expect(names.len()).to_equal(2)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        expect(names[0]).to_equal("pure_simple")
        expect(names[1]).to_equal("chromium")

    it "renders the showcase to a non-empty bitmap of the expected dimensions":
        step("Verify: renders the showcase to a non-empty bitmap of the expected dimensions")
        # @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001
        val ev = _lane_evidence()
        _report_lane("bitmap", ev)
        val pixels = _base_pixels()
        expect(pixels.len()).to_equal(SHOW_W * SHOW_H)
        val capture = backend_capture_ok(ev.lane, pixels, SHOW_W, SHOW_H)
        assert_true(capture.success)
        expect(capture.width).to_equal(SHOW_W)
        expect(capture.height).to_equal(SHOW_H)
        expect(capture.pixel_checksum).to_be_greater_than(0)
        # a rich showcase must not collapse to a flat frame
        expect(capture.unique_sampled_colors).to_be_greater_than(2)
        expect(capture.non_background_pixels).to_be_greater_than(0)
        print "showcase_capture lane={ev.lane} probe_source={ev.source} checksum={capture.pixel_checksum} unique_colors={capture.unique_sampled_colors}"

    it "carries the showcase palette into the bitmap (pixel probes)":
        step("Verify: carries the showcase palette into the bitmap (pixel probes)")
        # @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001
        _report_lane("palette", _lane_evidence())
        val pixels = _base_pixels()
        expect(_count_color(pixels, COLOR_BODY_BG)).to_be_greater_than(0)
        expect(_count_color(pixels, COLOR_BOX_BLUE)).to_be_greater_than(0)
        expect(_count_color(pixels, COLOR_BOX_GREEN)).to_be_greater_than(0)
        expect(_count_color(pixels, COLOR_BOX_AMBER)).to_be_greater_than(0)

    it "lays the flex row boxes out side by side on shared scanlines":
        step("Verify: lays the flex row boxes out side by side on shared scanlines")
        # @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001
        _report_lane("flex-row", _lane_evidence())
        val pixels = _base_pixels()
        assert_true(_row_has_both(pixels, COLOR_BOX_BLUE, COLOR_BOX_GREEN))
        assert_true(_row_has_both(pixels, COLOR_BOX_GREEN, COLOR_BOX_AMBER))

    it "produces a deterministic checksum across two identical renders":
        step("Verify: produces a deterministic checksum across two identical renders")
        # @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001
        _report_lane("determinism", _lane_evidence())
        val first = _base_pixels()
        val second = _render_showcase_pixels(_showcase_html())
        expect(_diffcount(first, second)).to_equal(0)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        val cap_a = backend_capture_ok("run_a", first, SHOW_W, SHOW_H)
        val cap_b = backend_capture_ok("run_b", second, SHOW_W, SHOW_H)
        expect(cap_a.pixel_checksum).to_equal(cap_b.pixel_checksum)

    it "changes the checksum when the document changes (mutation sensitivity)":
        step("Verify: changes the checksum when the document changes (mutation sensitivity)")
        # @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001
        _report_lane("mutation", _lane_evidence())
        val base = _base_pixels()
        val mutated = _mutated_pixels()
        expect(mutated.len()).to_equal(SHOW_W * SHOW_H)
        val cap_base = backend_capture_ok("base", base, SHOW_W, SHOW_H)
        val cap_mut = backend_capture_ok("mutated", mutated, SHOW_W, SHOW_H)
        assert_false(cap_base.pixel_checksum == cap_mut.pixel_checksum)
        # the mutated document really replaced the blue box with the red one
        expect(_count_color(mutated, COLOR_BOX_BLUE)).to_equal(0)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        expect(_count_color(mutated, COLOR_BOX_MUTATED)).to_be_greater_than(0)

    it "renders the animated element at two animation times without corruption":
        step("Verify: renders the animated element at two animation times without corruption")
        # @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001
        _report_lane("animation", _lane_evidence())
        val html = _showcase_html()
        val at0 = simple_web_render_html_to_readback_result_with_engine2d_backend_at_time(
            html, SHOW_W, SHOW_H, "auto", 0
        )
        val at500 = simple_web_render_html_to_readback_result_with_engine2d_backend_at_time(
            html, SHOW_W, SHOW_H, "auto", 500
        )
        expect(at0.readback.pixels.len()).to_equal(SHOW_W * SHOW_H)
        expect(at500.readback.pixels.len()).to_equal(SHOW_W * SHOW_H)
        # the animated pulse element paints at t=0
        expect(
            _count_color(at0.readback.pixels, 0xFFEF4444u32)
        ).to_be_greater_than(0)

    it "reports honest offload readback provenance for the executed lane":
        step("Verify: reports honest offload readback provenance for the executed lane")
        # @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001
        val ev = _lane_evidence()
        _report_lane("provenance", ev)
        # The SHOWCASE document on the named lane — not a retargeted CPU copy.
        val result = simple_web_render_html_to_readback_result_with_engine2d_backend(
            _showcase_html(), SHOW_W, SHOW_H, "auto"
        )
        val rb = result.readback
        expect(rb.pixels.len()).to_equal(SHOW_W * SHOW_H)
        assert_false(rb.source == "")
        print "showcase_readback lane={ev.lane} source={rb.source} handle={rb.backend_handle} identity={rb.device_identity}"
        # The same honest-provenance invariants the probe frame must satisfy
        # also bind the real showcase frame, so a lane cannot pass the probe and
        # then fabricate on the document.
        _assert_provenance_invariants("provenance-showcase", _LaneEvidence(
            lane: ev.lane, source: rb.source, handle: rb.backend_handle,
            identity: rb.device_identity,
            pixel_count: rb.pixels.len().to_i64()
        ))
        # A CPU-family lane must never claim a GPU device readback.
        if _is_cpu_family(ev.lane):
            assert_false(rb.source == "device_readback")
            expect(_source_is_cpu(rb.source)).to_equal(true)
        else:
            # A NAMED GPU lane must serve the showcase document from the device.
            # This is the assertion the old silent "software" retarget removed:
            # with it gone, resolved=cuda + source=cpu_fallback is red here
            # instead of a green CPU run under a "full GPU offload" title.
            expect(_source_is_device(rb.source)).to_equal(true)

    it "retains the showcase render in a session with full-parse counters":
        step("Verify: retains the showcase render in a session with full-parse counters")
        # @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001
        _report_lane("session-counters", _lane_evidence())
        val session = SimpleWebRenderSession.create()
        val overlay = browser_text_input_overlay_empty()
        val result = session.render(
            _snapshot(301, 1, 1, 1, Some(_showcase_html())),
            SHOW_W, SHOW_H, 0, 0, overlay, [], []
        )
        expect(result.raw_boxes.bx.len()).to_be_greater_than(0)
        expect(session.counters.serialize_count).to_equal(1)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        expect(session.counters.parse_count).to_equal(1)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        expect(session.counters.css_count).to_equal(1)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        expect(session.counters.layout_count).to_equal(1)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        expect(session.counters.paint_count).to_equal(1)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        expect(session.counters.composition_revision).to_equal(1)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        expect(session.counters.retained_node_count).to_be_greater_than(0)
        expect(session.counters.retained_command_count).to_be_greater_than(0)
        session.close()

    it "reuses the retained composition for an unchanged snapshot":
        step("Verify: reuses the retained composition for an unchanged snapshot")
        # @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001
        _report_lane("session-reuse", _lane_evidence())
        val session = SimpleWebRenderSession.create()
        val overlay = browser_text_input_overlay_empty()
        session.render(
            _snapshot(302, 1, 1, 1, Some(_showcase_html())),
            SHOW_W, SHOW_H, 0, 0, overlay, [], []
        )
        expect(session.counters.reuse_count).to_equal(0)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        session.render(
            _snapshot(302, 1, 1, 1, nil),
            SHOW_W, SHOW_H, 0, 0, overlay, [], []
        )
        expect(session.counters.reuse_count).to_equal(1)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        expect(session.counters.composition_revision).to_equal(1)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        session.close()

    it "keeps the session composition checksum stable until the document mutates":
        step("Verify: keeps the session composition checksum stable until the document mutates")
        # @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001
        _report_lane("session-checksum", _lane_evidence())
        # NOTE: mutation is proven across two sessions rather than a second
        # render on one session: under the seed interpreter, a second
        # full-layout render on the SAME session dies with "class
        # `WebLayoutManager` has no field named `bx`" — the `Some(retained)`
        # manager match arm in simple_web_render_session.spl leaks its binding
        # over the outer `retained` boxes var (known interpreter match-arm
        # shadowing defect; recorded as a blocking bug in this spec's report,
        # not masked here — the checksum contract itself is still verified).
        val overlay = browser_text_input_overlay_empty()
        val session_a = SimpleWebRenderSession.create()
        session_a.render(
            _snapshot(303, 1, 1, 1, Some(_showcase_html())),
            SHOW_W, SHOW_H, 0, 0, overlay, [], []
        )
        val first = session_a.composition_checksum()
        assert_false(first == "")
        # cache stability: repeated reads without a new render are identical
        expect(session_a.composition_checksum()).to_equal(first)
        session_a.close()
        val session_b = SimpleWebRenderSession.create()
        session_b.render(
            _snapshot(304, 1, 1, 1, Some(_mutated_html())),
            SHOW_W, SHOW_H, 0, 0, overlay, [], []
        )
        val second = session_b.composition_checksum()
        assert_false(second == "")
        # mutation sensitivity of the composition checksum
        assert_false(second == first)
        session_b.close()

    it "clears all retained state when the session closes":
        step("Verify: clears all retained state when the session closes")
        # @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001
        _report_lane("session-close", _lane_evidence())
        val session = SimpleWebRenderSession.create()
        val overlay = browser_text_input_overlay_empty()
        session.render(
            _snapshot(304, 1, 1, 1, Some(_showcase_html())),
            SHOW_W, SHOW_H, 0, 0, overlay, [], []
        )
        session.close()
        expect(session.composition_checksum()).to_equal("")
        expect(session.counters.retained_node_count).to_equal(0)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        expect(session.counters.retained_command_count).to_equal(0)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract

    it "keeps the web backend facade honest about engines and inputs":
        step("Verify: keeps the web backend facade honest about engines and inputs")
        # @req: REQ-GUI-WebShowFullGpuOffl-001
        _report_lane("facade", _lane_evidence())
        _arm_showcase_budget()
        # alias normalization
        expect(web_render_backend("blink", 8, 8).name()).to_equal("chromium")
        expect(web_render_backend("bogus", 8, 8).name()).to_equal("pure_simple")
        # chromium has no Draw IR: empty composition, never pure_simple output
        val chromium = web_render_backend("chromium", SHOW_W, SHOW_H)
        val chromium_ir = chromium.render_html_to_draw_ir(_showcase_html())
        expect(chromium_ir.batches.len()).to_equal(0)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        # pure_simple produces a real Draw IR command list for the showcase
        val pure = web_render_backend("pure_simple", SHOW_W, SHOW_H)
        val ir = pure.render_html_to_draw_ir(_showcase_html())
        var commands = 0
        for batch in ir.batches:
            commands = commands + batch.commands.len()
        expect(commands).to_be_greater_than(0)
        # software ground-truth path also renders the full bitmap
        val soft = pure.render_html_software_pixels(_showcase_html())
        expect(soft.len()).to_equal(SHOW_W * SHOW_H)
        # missing file yields an empty buffer, not garbage
        val missing = pure.render_html_file_to_pixels(
            "build/tmp/does_not_exist_showcase.html", "auto"
        )
        expect(missing.len()).to_equal(0)  # oracle: value fixed by the spec contract
        # pure_simple has no live window; caller must present pixels itself
        assert_false(pure.show_live_window("x.html", 32, 32, "showcase"))
        # ------------------------------------------------------------------
        # Run-level disclosure. A green in which no device ever served a frame
        # must not be readable as a green that proved GPU offload — the exact
        # confusion this suite shipped for weeks. The evidence is the
        # per-example receipt LINES above, not a cross-example tally: a module
        # `var` counter and a class field both read back stale here, which is
        # why the retarget's own `probe_source=` disclosure printed empty.
        # These verdict lines carry a DIFFERENT prefix from the receipts, so
        # they can name the tokens without ever being counted as evidence.
        # ------------------------------------------------------------------
        print "[showcase-verdict] this run DECLARES {SHOWCASE_EXAMPLES_DECLARED} examples, each emitting exactly one '[showcase-lane] <label>: ...' receipt."
        print "[showcase-verdict] count the receipts with ANCHORED greps: `grep -c '^.showcase-lane. '` (total), `grep -c '^.showcase-lane. .*GPU-PROVEN'`, `grep -c '^.showcase-lane. .*DEVICE-SERVED'`, `grep -c '^.showcase-lane. .*CPU LANE'`. Anchor at line start — an unanchored grep counts this verdict's own prose."
        print "[showcase-verdict] GPU-PROVEN + DEVICE-SERVED is the number of examples that ran with a device behind them. CPU LANE receipts prove NOTHING about GPU offload."
        print "[showcase-verdict] a PASS whose receipts are all 'CPU LANE' is INCONCLUSIVE for GPU offload, NOT a GPU offload pass — read it as 'no viable device on this host', never as 'the offload path works'. The pixel, layout, session and facade evidence in such a run is still real."
        print "[showcase-verdict] a NAMED GPU lane that serves a CPU frame is NOT inconclusive — it is a hard failure (LANE INTEGRITY FAILURE), because detect_best_backend_viable commits only to a provably working backend. That case used to be silently retargeted to 'software' and reported 13 examples, 0 failures."
